{"seq": 71, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #87; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": ", clarifying #43,#49,#52,#63 without changing their tables: for model m on edge e, u_em=b_m+c_em h_em and independently sampled S_m shots give Var(mean_m uhat_em)=(1/M^2) sum_m c_em^2(1-h_em^2)/S_m, where h_em=(u_em-b_m)/c_em. Classical biases are added exactly. With equal per-model S and c, total shots per edge T=M S and variance=c^2(1-mean_m h_em^2)/T; the effective scale at per-model S is c_eff=c/sqrt(M). Existing grids instead are literally one synthetic estimator: c_grid, bias zero, S_grid total shots per edge and 120 S_grid per trial, overlap u_export/c_grid. They are not per-model resource counts. Only in the additional identical-model, zero-bias assumption can this single binomial equal pooled M models with common c=c_grid and S_grid/M shots each, total T=S_grid (integer allocation required). If S_grid were instead allocated to each of 45 equal-c models, total T=45 S_grid and c_eff=c_grid/sqrt(45), a different variance and distribution from the saved tables. Thus each saved grid is relabelled synthetic total-shot budget, not a 45-model threshold; no universal equal-c ensemble interpretation exists for heterogeneous overlaps. Nonzero actual bias 0.41671419 from #48 requires h=(u-b)/c and is not represented by those b=0 grids. Existing numbers remain unchanged; a heterogeneous per-model rerun is the required continuation."}
