  "emmy/final-precision-account.md": "# Conditional ensemble costs and blind common bias

Working research account. This extends joint ledger entries 16 and 20-23.
It is neither a publication draft nor a quantum advantage claim.

The supported pair-specific line is a matched-function approximate-inference
audit whose physical endpoint is ligand binding-free-energy accuracy at a
declared fixed simulation allocation. The saved classical functions must be
reproduced before converting score tolerances into quantum resources. A frozen
classically learned row re-encoding does not test quantum-origin training;
joint Ada entry 22 correctly separates those experiments.

## New allocation calculation

Inherited branch-2 ledger entries 73, 81 and 91 provide conditional arrays
for 45 models, twelve substitute mappings and 120 candidate edges. Joint
entry 10 checks their availability and their failure to reproduce historical
exported predictions. The new calculation in emmy/ensemble_allocation_audit.py
uses those saved arrays without rerunning inference. Its output and hashes
are in emmy/ensemble-allocation-diagnostic.json.

For fixed edge and mapping, write the model output as
\(u_m=b_m+c_mh_m\), with classical bias \(b_m\), norm product \(c_m\),
and real overlap \(h_m\). Independent unbiased Hadamard shots give
\[
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\bar u})
=\frac{1}{M^2}\sum_m\frac{v_m}{S_m},
\qquad v_m=c_m^2(1-h_m^2).
\]
For known positive per-shot costs \(k_m\) and total cost
\(K=\sum_m k_mS_m\), standard Cauchy-Schwarz gives
\[
\sum_m\frac{c_m^2}{S_m}
\ge\frac{(\sum_m c_m\sqrt{k_m})^2}{K}.
\]
Equality occurs at \(S_m\propto c_m/\sqrt{k_m}\). Variance-optimal
allocation replaces \(c_m\) by \(\sqrt{v_m}\). Integer constraints,
fixed circuit setup costs and pilots for unknown overlaps are excluded.
This is an extension of the inherited standard allocation identities in
branches/branch-1 ledger entry 96 and branches/branch-2 ledger entry 28,
not a new allocation algorithm. A synthetic nonuniform cost schedule checks
the identity numerically; it supplies no estimated device times.

For equal per-shot costs, the norm allocation improves the conditional
worst-case coefficient over equal model shots by median factor 1.4385,
with 5th/95th percentiles 1.1698/1.9628 across mapping-edge coordinates.
Oracle standard-deviation allocation has median factor 1.4439. These
conditional improvements cannot repair missing historical inputs, turn
branch-1 proxy thresholds into recovered-graph budgets, or eliminate bias.
The twelve-candidate maximum-norm envelope bounds only sampled candidates.

Joint Ada entry 21 independently adds exact classical member subsampling.
For model population variance \(v_{\rm members}\), uniform exact-member
sampling with replacement has variance \(v_{\rm members}/k\); without
replacement it has variance
\((M-k)v_{\rm members}/[k(M-1)]\). I inspected
ada/ensemble_subsampling_audit.py and ada/ensemble-subsampling-control.md:
the bias handling, finite-population formula and enumeration of all 990 pairs
for one coordinate match that claim. This is checking saved source arithmetic,
not independent inference. The present script also compares oracle optimised
quantum variance coefficients with this classical member variance. These are
larger at all 1440 conditional coordinates, with ratio minimum 4.39869 and
median 197.27762. This comparison includes oracle variance-optimal allocation,
so the ordering here does not depend on equal quantum shot allocation.
They remain
different computational units and probability laws. A variance ratio is
neither a runtime ratio nor a planner-quality comparison; encoder and native
execution costs and joint error laws must still be measured.

## Common bias invisible to graph comparison

Inherited branch-1 ledger entries 32-33 and joint entry 9 use centred fit maps
\(G,H\), common candidate incidence \(B\), and \(D=H-G\), for which
\(GB=HB=C_0\) and \(DB=0\). For any common ligand-potential error
\(Bz\), both graphs shift by \(C_0z\), while their difference is unchanged:
\[
D(d+Bz)=Dd,
\qquad (I-BG)Bz=0.
\]
Thus cycle closure also cannot see this error component. Under covariance
\(\Sigma=B C B^{\mathsf T}\), graph-difference covariance is zero while
baseline ligand covariance is \(C_0 C C_0\). This standard nullspace
counterexample is a concrete limitation of stability-based precision claims.
It is not an estimate of actual force-field errors.

emmy/common_bias_audit.py tests the identity on the conditionally recovered
RBFENN27 and LOMAP23 graphs in
branches/branch-3/ada/selected-edge-recovery.json, using the inherited
inverse-variance convention. A deliberately artificial centred ligand error
aligned with baseline experimental residuals has mean absolute magnitude
1 kcal/mol. Adding its candidate-edge differences raises deterministic
RBFENN MUE from 0.6018276 to 1.6018276 and LOMAP MUE from 0.5526226
to 1.5229408. Graph-comparison and cycle-residual changes remain below
\(2.4\times10^{-14}\). Records and input hashes are in
emmy/common-bias-diagnostic.json. The example uses experimental residuals
to construct an adverse error; it does not use experiments for graph selection
and offers no prospective calibration. These point fits are different from
the author bootstrap median bars near 0.66-0.67 audited in joint entries 4-6.

Q already distinguishes precise estimates from systematically inaccurate
ones in branches/branch-3/inputs/Q.txt:203-208. The combined implication is stricter than requiring
topology stability or graph-change risk alone: the baseline must have
independently validated physical accuracy. Common bias must be calibrated or
bounded separately. Approximate inference should preserve that baseline under
a declared physical-outcome coupling, with mean and worst-ligand error reported.

## Practical decision

The next useful work is faithful classical input/prediction reproduction,
followed by a measured function-controlled precision/error/cost comparison.
Include exact circuit-family evaluation, matched binomial controls, lower
precision classical evaluation and ensemble subsampling. If the question is
training, optimise the same angle coordinates, optimiser and joint noise law;
free-weight continuation is a separate family. Native state loading, gradients,
shots, extraction, correlations and device bias must enter the cost comparison.

No present result establishes a competitive hardware pilot, prospective
accuracy preservation, novelty or a publication-ready positive finding.
The common-bias construction and conditional cost controls make a useful
negative audit more precise. Historical mappings and activations, calibrated
joint physical errors and bias, constraint-preserving fixed allocation,
typeset publication comparison, independent targets and full prior-work
coverage remain unresolved.
